\b 5. Project files and local storage\b0\par
The Software saves project files where you choose and periodically writes an autosave/recovery project under your local application data directory, normally %LocalAppData%\\NorthstarNetworkLab\\autosave.nslab. Preferences, including an optional Discord Application ID, are stored separately in that local directory. You are responsible for backups, access permissions, and deleting files you no longer need.\par
\par
The current Software contains no Provider-operated cloud project service, account service, or analytics/telemetry service. Windows, your organization, backup software, or synchronization tools may separately copy local files under their own settings and policies.\par
\par
\b 6. Optional Discord Rich Presence\b0\par
Rich Presence is optional and disabled until configured. If enabled, the Software connects to Discord Desktop through a local IPC channel and submits activity fields such as project name, selected device name/type, or current simulation action so Discord can display the activity. It does not intentionally send topology contents, interface addresses, or project files through Rich Presence. Discord's handling of activity data is governed by Discord's own terms and privacy policy. You can disconnect and clear the saved Application ID in Software settings.\par
\par
\b 7. Multiplayer and network communications\b0\par
Multiplayer is optional and starts only when a user chooses to host or connect. It uses direct TCP communication between peers. A joining peer sends a display name; the host sends project snapshots; peers send authorized project updates, lock requests, and presence/leave messages. Project snapshots can contain device names, interface addresses, routes, services, wireless settings, notes, annotations, IoT state, and lab activities.\par
\par
The current multiplayer implementation does not provide transport encryption, peer authentication, account identity verification, a relay, or automatic NAT traversal. A host accepts connections on the configured listening port. Anyone able to reach that port may attempt to connect. Edit locks coordinate editing but are not a security boundary against a malicious peer. Use multiplayer only on a network you trust, restrict inbound firewall access to the intended private network, and do not expose this port to the public internet. Disconnect if an unexpected peer joins.\par
